hi there Steve Kaufman here today to  talk about today I'm going to talk about  repetition and novelty and if you enjoy  my videos on language learning please  subscribe by the way I am closing in on  100,000 subscribers so I don't know what  I'll do and I hit that number maybe I'll  go out with my wife and or will open a  bottle of champagne at home but it's  been fun over the past 10 years I've  really really enjoyed doing this because  it's part of what I'm interested in  which is learning languages over the  past 10 years while I've been making  these videos I think I've learned seven  or eight languages and now I'm on to  Greek as you know so it's very much part  of what I'm doing and what I enjoy doing  and I like sharing my experience with  others and my experience does also  include a reading on how languages are  required so repetition um obviously  repetition is good you need to see words  many times before they stick you need to  see certain patterns in the language  many times before they start to become  part of you know what you're comfortable  doing but as I have pointed out before  when I mentioned the work of Robert  Bjork who talks about interleaving we  can't just focus on sort of a limited  amount of data and just try to master it  because you read it over and over again  hoping that it's going to stick in our  brain it won't it's it's you're going  over the same ground all the time and  every time you go over the same ground  you're picking up less and less so it's  very important to vary that with novelty  I often quote Manfred Spitzer the famous  German neuroscientist who said to learn  the brain needs repetition and novelty  you have to feed the brain something new  you know and I'm assuming people are  curious anyone who isn't curious for  know--they isn't going to learn anyone  who thinks they can simply by rote  memory and by not being curious about  the language how the line with language  works  curious about things in the culture that  person simply isn't going to learn so  our very well so so plain repetition  rote memory although there are people  who swear by it I'm going to memorize  the most important thousand words are  going to memorize that grammar ace the  grammar I have never found that that  works  and I noticed that recently because with  my Greek I've been doing our mini  stories and I've not got up to 40 mini  stories that we have loaded up at linked  audio and text and but because our  writer who's writing these stories has  now slowed down we don't have any new  stories and therefore they have been  translated and so I am going over the  same stories over and over and over  again because the same newspaper  articles that I can find on the Internet  are still too difficult for me for Greek  so I stay with these stories but I'm  realizing that I'm getting less and less  out of them and that when I go and do a  newspaper article or something more  difficult and I come back to the stories  again the simple stories I get a little  more again because I varied it a bit so  it's very important to introduce that  novelty and it does not just apply to  that and and also not only novelty but  that sense that you're moving forward  that you're achieving so if I read  something on link where there are a lot  of blue words and I'm converting them to  yellow I'm looking them up then I have  the sense that I'm achieving something  right if I'm going over the same stuff  over and over and over again hoping that  it's kind of gradually you know cause my  neuron neurons to fire together but  after a while I think there's just no  novelty there and so the return on  investment is less and less and this  applies to many things it applies to  grammar get 3 grammar resources read  them bolt read them all and cover the  same ground from three different angles  before we had link I used to buy three  starter books teacher self colloquial  level living language cover the same  ground from three different angles or  borrow them from the library in other  words the greater the variety that you  can introduce to your repetition the  more fresh then that repetition is going  to be and I even find that when I go say  in my Greek and I do something very  difficult I'm struggling yourself when I  go back then to my Greek stories I'm  picking up more and another thing that  I've done talking again about variety  and repetition because the Greek stories  the original  mornings of them were very fast and in  some ways it was good because it made me  you know more comfortable with someone  speaking quickly but then I thought  maybe some of our learners would be  discouraged by having the audio so fast  so I asked Pablo Saar a Greek translator  and fellow who's doing the recordings to  do them again more slowly and now I  listen to them again slowly and I'm  picking up a lot more so just varying  the speeds is another form of  introducing novelty variety so I think  that's extremely important not to rely  on rote memory or any you know  expectation that by really focusing and  learning and and repeating that somehow  things are going to stick you have to  follow Manfred Spitzer's advice you have  to mix up the repetition with the  novelty so there you have it thank you  for listening bye for now  [Music]  you  [Music]
 

